</ul><h1>The Great Divide</h1><p>Harriet brought her lunch to the table in the break room, joining Gale and Martin.</p>
<p>“How about that huddle this morning?” she said, blowing on a forkful of hot salad. “If most meetings could be an email, that one could’ve been a text message, amirite?”</p>
<p>Gale and Martin exchanged a look. “Harriet, did you just microwave salad?”</p>
<p>“Oh, this?” Harriet looked at the forkful. “Yeah. But it’s a ‘lasagna salad’. So it’s supposed to be eaten warm.”</p>
<p>“A lasagna salad,” Martin said, unpacking his sandwich.</p>
<p>“And remind us what you had yesterday?” Gale said, grabbing a piece of sushi with chopsticks.</p>
<p>“Pot roast with mashed potato and carrot sauce,” Harriet said.</p>
<p>“And for your snack this morning?” Martin said.</p>
<p>“Just a little veggie, hummus, and Cheez-it medley,” Harriet said. “Why? What’s going on? It seems like you two want to say something to me.”</p>
<p>“It’s just…” Martin began to say, but trailed off.</p>
<p>Gale sighed. “I’ll say it. Harriet, you don’t need to pretend that your meals are intentional.”</p>
<p>“What do you mean?” Harriet said, feigning confusion.</p>
<p>“We mean, you pack your lunches and snacks into single-compartment containers,” Martin said, “and then when they inevitably get mixed up, you act like you meant it to be that way.”</p>
<p>Harriet glanced around nervously. “I don’t know what you’re talking about.”</p>
<p>“Look, Harriet,” Gale said. “You should get some Bento Box 44oz To-go Containers. They’ve got a whole bunch of different pieces and compartments, so you can pack each part of your meal separately.”</p>
<p>“Yeah, check it out.” Martin took out his phone. “They’re selling a three-pack on Meh today. And they come with ice packs.”</p>
<p>Harriet looked. “Oh, interesting. Maybe I’ll get those. But I just want you to know, all my meals <em>are</em> intentional, okay? Today’s, yesterday’s, and tomorrow’s: my famous spaghetti, meatballs, and chocolate chip cookie surprise.”</p>
<p>“Eww,” Martin said.</p>
<p>“But sure, fine,” Gale said. “Whatever you need to tell yourself.”</p>